edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186358947 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360319 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b8b075c5-1dd5-565f-a18b-3f5b7c1118ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360432 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360432 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186358603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360432 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360432 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360432 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186359648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360432 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360432 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360432 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186358603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360432 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f7663cb0-dd8e-5af0-842c-4853b4c5638f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360459 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360459 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94612" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360459 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360459 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2,$ and its subset $B=\\{p(t)\\in {R}_{2}[t]\\,|\\,p(1)=2\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$\n\nIV. $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360459 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef92c245-5042-5dba-9942-02e46fcd2132" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360459 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(1)=g(1)=2,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1)+g(1) & =2+2 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(1)=2$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(1) & =2\\cdot 2=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360460 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360460 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94615" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360460 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360460 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{4}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $4,$ and its subset $B=\\{p(t)\\in {R}_{4}[t]\\,|\\,p(0)=1\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{4}[t]$\n\nIV. $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{4}[t]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360460 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec5fca83-ae3c-5f26-9292-3c61fcf8876c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360460 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(0)=g(0)=1,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0)+g(0) & =1+1 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(0)=1$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(0) & =1\\cdot 2=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360464 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360464 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94631" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360464 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360464 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $H=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H$ is closed under matrix addition\n\nII. $H$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $H$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n\nIV. $H$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360464 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10fb8ec2-9c2e-55ae-85b0-700b4fea54e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360464 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}={A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $H.$ So, $H$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =2[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $H.$ So, $H$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $H$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360465 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360465 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94632" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360465 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360465 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $L$ containing all polynomials of degree exactly $1.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $L$ is closed under function addition\n\nII. $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $L$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n\nIV. $L$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360465 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6847dae1-289b-5050-b4ae-95ca49b57e85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360465 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=1-x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)+g(x) & =x+(1-x) \\\\  & =(x-x)+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)=x$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot x=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $L$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $L$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360466 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360466 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94633" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360466 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360466 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $Q$ containing all polynomials of degree exactly $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $Q$ is closed under function addition\n\nII. $Q$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $Q$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n\nIV. $Q$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360466 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ee30622-b701-5da4-9a44-4e5ce669fd87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360466 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $g(x)=-{x}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nf(x)+g(x)={x}^{2}+(-{x}^{2})=0.\n$$\n\nSo, $Q$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot {x}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $Q$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $Q$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446381 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446381 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446381 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02490bd0-71b5-513c-b520-c0758ef51227" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446382 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II,   and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446382 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446382 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3829f03a-07c9-5578-b4ae-972b6075fce1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446383 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446383 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446383 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "40374cb8-c538-5fe0-94c9-123e108a0c2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446384 #edb/r