lar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if  $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=-x,$ then $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are in $B,$ but\n\n$$\n(f+g)(x)=x-x=0\n$$\n\n for all $x\\in [0,1].$\nIn particular, \n\n$$\n(f+g)(1)=(f+g)(-1).\n$$\n\n So, $f+g\\notin B.$ Hence, $B$ is *not* closed under function addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $f(x)\\in B$ and $k=0,$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(1) & =0\\cdot f(1) \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & =0\\cdot f(-1) \\\\  & =(kf)(-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $kf\\notin B.$ Hence, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446400 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446400 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107103" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446400 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446400 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2,$ and its subset $A$ containing all polynomials of the form $at-b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $A$ is a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$\n\nIV. $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446400 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "645fccc2-d171-5fdf-83e7-9f6a35f005d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446400 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)={a}_{1}t-{b}_{1}$ and $g(t)={a}_{2}t-{b}_{2}$ are two polynomials from $A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}t-{b}_{1})+({a}_{2}t-{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})t-({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=at-b\\in A$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =k(at-b) \\\\  & =(ka)t-(kb),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446401 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446401 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107145" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446401 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446401 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $G=\\{f\\in C[0,1]\\,|\\,f(0)\\ge 0\\}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $G$ is closed under function addition\n\nII. $G$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $G$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n\nIV. $G$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446401 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e86fe7b-82ad-5786-81ac-5ea87b6be543" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446401 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, if $f(x),g(x)\\in G,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(0) & =f(0)+g(0) \\\\  & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $G$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in [0,1]$ and $k=-1\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(kf)(0) & =((-1)f)(0) \\\\  & =-f(0) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $G$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $G$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
